@node Naming system
@section Naming system

In COBOL, user-defined words (variables, procedure names, etc.) use @code{case-insensitive} naming (conventionally @code{UPPER_CASE} or @code{mixed-case} with hyphens).
All keywords are case-insensitive but conventionally written in uppercase.

@table @asis
@item Keywords: ACCEPT, ADD, CALL, CANCEL, COMPUTE, DELETE, DISPLAY, DIVIDE, ENTRY, EVALUATE, IF, MOVE, MULTIPLY, OPEN, PERFORM, READ, RETURN, REWRITE, SEARCH, SET, SORT, START, STOP, STRING, SUBTRACT, UNSTRING, WRITE
@item Operators: +, -, *, /, **, =, >, <, >=, <=, <> (or NOT=), AND, OR, NOT
@item String delimiters: Quotation marks (@code{"..."}) or apostrophes (@code{'...'}) for non-numeric literals
@item Comment lines: Indicated by an asterisk (*) in the indicator column (column 7) or @code{*>} anywhere after column 7
@end table

@node Basic structure
@section Basic structure for a stand-alone routine or program

A COBOL program consists of four divisions: Identification, Environment, Data, and Procedure.
The Procedure division contains the executable statements.

@example
       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
       PROGRAM-ID. GAMELOGIC.

       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.
       CONFIGURATION SECTION.
       SOURCE-COMPUTER. RICOH-RP2A03.
       OBJECT-COMPUTER. RICOH-RP2A03.

       DATA DIVISION.
       FILE SECTION.
       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
       01  GAME-STATE.
           05  SCORE            PIC 9(6) VALUE 0.
           05  LIVES-LEFT       PIC 9    VALUE 3.
           05  CURRENT-ROOM     PIC 9(2) VALUE 1.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.
       MAIN-PROGRAM.
           PERFORM INITIALIZE-GAME
           PERFORM MAIN-LOOP UNTIL GAME-OVER
           STOP RUN.

       INITIALIZE-GAME.
           MOVE 0 TO SCORE
           MOVE 3 TO LIVES-LEFT
           MOVE 1 TO CURRENT-ROOM.

       MAIN-LOOP.
           PERFORM PROCESS-INPUT
           PERFORM UPDATE-GAME
           PERFORM RENDER-DISPLAY
           IF LIVES-LEFT = 0
               SET GAME-OVER TO TRUE
           END-IF.

       PROCESS-INPUT.
           ACCEPT PLAYER-INPUT
           IF PLAYER-INPUT = "MOVE NORTH"
               ADD 10 TO CURRENT-ROOM
           END-IF.

       UPDATE-GAME.
           @r{Game logic updates}

       RENDER-DISPLAY.
           @r{Rendering code}
@end example

@node Basic structure object
@section Basic structure for an object method

COBOL supports object-oriented programming through factory and instance methods defined in the CLASS division.

@example
       CLASS-ID. PLAYER-GAME.
       INHERITS BASIC-OBJECT.

       FACTORY.
       METHOD-ID. CREATE-INSTANCE.
           PROCEDURE DIVISION USING RETURNING OBJ AS OBJECT REFERENCE.
           SET OBJ TO SELF
           EXIT METHOD.
       END METHOD.

       OBJECT.
       METHOD-ID. MOVE-PLAYER.
           PROCEDURE DIVISION USING DX AS INTEGER DY AS INTEGER.
           COMPUTE SELF/X = SELF/X + DX
           COMPUTE SELF/Y = SELF/Y + DY
           EXIT METHOD.
       END METHOD.
       END CLASS.
@end example

@node Calling routines
@section Calling a routine (nullary)

To call a procedure that takes no arguments, use the CALL statement or PERFORM for internal procedures:

@example
       CALL "INITIALIZE-GRAPHICS"
       CALL "CLEAR-SCREEN"
       CALL "PLAY-STARTUP-MUSIC"
       PERFORM INITIALIZE-GAME
@end example

@node Calling routines value
@section Calling a routine with a value (in accumulator)

To call a function that returns a single value (using RETURNING):

@example
       MOVE FUNCTION NUMVAL(FUNCTION ACCEPT()) TO SCORE
       COMPUTE BONUS = FUNCTION CALCULATE-BONUS(LEVEL, TIME-LEFT)
       COMPpute VOLUME = FUNCTION GET-MASTER-VOLUME()
@end example

@node Calling routines giving
@section Calling a routine giving a value (in accumulator)

Not applicable in standard COBOL; use RETURNING in the called procedure.

@node Calling routines values
@section Calling a routine with values in and out (in accumulator)

To call a procedure with parameters:

@example
       CALL "DRAW-SPRITE" USING SPRITE-ID XPOS YPOS
       CALL "PLAY-SOUND" USING SOUND-ID VOLUME LOOP-FLAG
       CALL "CHECK-COLLISION" USING OBJ1 OBJ2 GIVING RESULT-VAR
       PERFORM UPDATE-PLAYER USING PLAYER-X PLAYER-Y
@end example

@node Calling library
@section Calling a library routine (all 4 styles)

COBOL accesses library routines through CALL statements or intrinsic functions:

@example
       CALL "LOADLEVEL" USING LEVEL-NUMBER          @r{Direct call}
       CALL "FARPROC" USING LIBRARY-ADDRESS         @r{Far call}
       COMPUTE RESULT = FUNCTION LIBRARY-FUNCTION(INPUT)  @r{Library function}
       INVOKE OBJECT "DISPLAY" METHOD "SHOW-MESSAGE" USING MESSAGE-ID  @r{Object method call}
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: CALL "ProcedureName"(...) or PERFORM ProcedureName
@item Far: CALL "AbsoluteAddress"(...) (requires linker support)
@item Library: CALL "LibraryEntry"(...) or FunctionName(...)
@item Object: INVOKE OBJECT ... METHOD ... USING ...
@end table

@node Calling far bank
@section Calling a far bank Service (all 4 styles)

Far procedures in COBOL are called using absolute addresses or through
the linkage configuration for inter-bank calls:

@example
       CALL "FAR-LOAD-LIBRARY" USING &H8000         @r{Call routine in bank 1}
       CALL "EXT-PROC" USING SEGMENT-VALUE OFFSET   @r{Call procedure in another segment}
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Not used for far calls
@item Far: CALL with segment:offset or far pointer
@item Library: Not applicable
@item Object: Not applicable
@end table

@node Invoking object
@section Invoking a method on an object (all 4 styles)

Object method invocation in COBOL uses the INVOKE statement:

@example
       INVOKE OBJECT "PLAYER" METHOD "MOVE-PLAYER" USING 5 10
       INVOKE OBJECT "SCORE-DISPLAY" METHOD "UPDATE" USING SCORE
       INVOKE OBJECT "GAME-STATE" METHOD "IS-GAME-OVER" RETURNING GAME-OVER-FLAG
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Setting object properties via MOVE or COMPUTE
@item Far: Not applicable for methods (objects typically in same bank)
@item Library: Method may call library routines internally
@item Object: METHOD invocation on object instance
@end table

@node Assignment
@section Assigning to a variable

Variables are assigned using MOVE or COMPUTE statements:

@example
       MOVE 0 TO SCORE                           @r{Explicit assignment}
       MOVE 3 TO LIVES-LEFT                      @r{Implicit assignment}
       ADD 1 TO COUNTER                          @r{Increment}
       SUBTRACT 1 FROM LIVES                     @r{Decrement}
       COMPUTE SCORE = SCORE + 10                @r{Arithmetic assignment}
       MOVE "Hello, World!" TO MESSAGE           @r{String assignment}
@end example

@node Assignment object
@section Assigning to an object slot

Object slots are assigned using MODIFY or INVOKE with SET property:

@example
       MODIFY SELF/X = 160                       @r{Set object's x coordinate}
       MODIFY SELF/Y = 100                       @r{Set object's y coordinate}
       MOVE 5 TO SELF/SPRITE                     @r{Set sprite number}
       INVOKE OBJECT "PLAYER" METHOD "SET-VISIBLE" USING TRUE
       @r{Set visibility flag}
@end example

@node Assignment array
@section Assigning to an array member with a subscript

Array elements are assigned using indexing with parentheses or INDEXED BY:

@example
       01  SCORE-TABLE.
           05  SCORE-ENTRY OCCURS 10 TIMES.
               10  INITIALS  PIC XXX.
               10  POINTS    PIC 9(6).

       01  BOARD.
           05  ROW OCCURS 8 TIMES INDEXED BY ROW-IDX.
               10  COL OCCURS 8 TIMES INDEXED BY COL-IDX.
                   15  CELL PIC 9.

       MOVE "ALI" TO INITIALS OF SCORE-ENTRY OF SCORE-TABLE(1)
       MOVE 100 TO POINTS OF SCORE-ENTRY OF SCORE-TABLE(1)
       MOVE 1 TO CELL OF ROW(3) OF COL(4) OF BOARD
@end example

@node Arithmetic
@section Basic arithmetic: addition and subtraction

Basic arithmetic uses ADD, SUBTRACT, or COMPUTE statements:

@example
       ADD 10 TO SCORE                           @r{Addition}
       SUBTRACT 1 FROM LIVES-LEFT                @r{Subtraction}
       ADD WIDTH HEIGHT GIVING TOTAL             @r{Add two variables}
       SUBTRACT DISCOUNT FROM PRICE GIVING FINAL-PRICE  @r{Subtraction}
       COMPUTE DELTA = NEW-VALUE - OLD-VALUE     @r{Subtraction}
@end example

@node Arithmetic bit
@section Basic arithmetic: bit-shifting for mutiplication or division

Bit-shifting for multiplication/division by powers of two is not directly supported.
Use COMPUTE with multiplication/division or implement via loops:

@example
       COMPUTE RESULT = VALUE * 4                @r{Multiply by 2^2}
       COMPUTE RESULT = VALUE / 2                @r{Divide by 2^1}
       COMPUTE RESULT = VALUE * 8                @r{Multiply by 2^3}
       COMPUTE RESULT = VALUE / 16               @r{Divide by 2^4}
@end example

@node Complex arithmetic
@section Complex arithmetic: multiple expressions

Complex arithmetic expressions use COMPUTE with operator precedence:

@example
       COMPUTE TOTAL = (BASE-SALARY * 12) + BONUS - DEDUCTIONS
       COMPUTE AVERAGE = (SUM1 + SUM2 + SUM3) / 3
       COMPUTE DISTANCE = FUNCTION SQRT(
                         (X2 - X1) ** 2 + (Y2 - Y1) ** 2)
@end example

@node Boolean
@section Boolean expressions: equality and inequalities

Boolean expressions use relation conditions in IF, EVALUATE, and PERFORM:

@example
       IF SCORE > 1000 THEN ...                  @r{Greater than}
       IF SCORE < 50 THEN ...                    @r{Less than}
       IF SCORE = 100 THEN ...                   @r{Equality}
       IF SCORE NOT = 0 THEN ...                 @r{Inequality}
       IF X >= 0 AND X < MAX-X THEN ...          @r{Combined conditions}
       IF BUTTON = BUTTON-A OR BUTTON = BUTTON-B THEN ... @r{Alternative conditions}
       EVALUATE TRUE ALSO
           WHEN SCORE > 1000 ALSO BUTTON = FIRE
               PERFORM FIRE-WEAPON
           WHEN SCORE < 50 ALSO BUTTON = LEFT
               PERFORM MOVE-LEFT
       END-EVALUATE
@end example

@node If then else
@section Using if/then/else structures and examine/cond structures

Conditional execution uses IF/THEN/ELSE/END-IF or EVALUATE statements:

@example
       IF TEMPERATURE > THOT THEN
           MOVE "HOT" TO STATUS
       ELSE IF TEMPERATURE > TCOLD THEN
           MOVE "WARM" TO STATUS
       ELSE
           MOVE "COLD" TO STATUS
       END-IF

       EVALUATE ANSWER
           WHEN "Y" OR "y"
               MOVE "YES" TO RESPONSE
           WHEN "N" OR "n"
               MOVE "NO" TO RESPONSE
           WHEN OTHER
               MOVE "INVALID" TO RESPONSE
       END-EVALUATE
@end example

COBOL does not have an EXAMINE/COND structure; EVALUATE serves
a similar purpose for multi-way branching.

@node Performing
@section Performing loops

Loops are implemented using PERFORM with TEST BEFORE/AFTER or inline PERFORM:

@example
       *> FOR/NEXT loop equivalent (known iterations)
       PERFORM VARYING I FROM 1 BY 1 UNTIL I > 10
           DISPLAY "Count: " I
       END-PERFORM

       *> WHILE/WEND loop equivalent (pre-test)
       PERFORM WITH TEST BEFORE UNTIL END-OF-FILE = TRUE
           READ TRANSACTION-FILE
               AT END SET END-OF-FILE TO TRUE
           NOT AT END
               PROCESS-TRANSACTION
       END-PERFORM

       *> DO/LOOP UNTIL equivalent (post-test)
       PERFORM WITH TEST AFTER UNTIL USER-RESPONSE = "Y"
           ACCEPT USER-RESPONSE
       END-PERFORM

       *> Inline PERFORM…END-PERFORM (structured block)
       PERFORM
           MOVE 0 TO COUNTER
           ADD 1 TO COUNTER
           IF COUNTER > 5 THEN EXIT PERFORM END-IF
       END-PERFORM
@end example

@node Breaking loops
@section Breaking out of loops safely

Early loop exit is achieved using EXIT PERFORM or EXIT DO variants:

@example
       *> Exit PERFORM loop early
       PERFORM VARYING I FROM 1 BY 1 UNTIL I > 100
           IF ARRAY(I) = TARGET-VALUE THEN
               DISPLAY "Found at index: " I
               EXIT PERFORM                    @r{Exit loop immediately}
           END-IF
       END-PERFORM

       *> Exit DO loop equivalent early
       PERFORM WITH TEST AFTER
           ACCEPT "Command> " COMMAND
           IF COMMAND = "QUIT" THEN
               EXIT PERFORM                    @r{Exit loop immediately}
           END-IF
           CALL PROCESS-COMMAND(COMMAND)
       END-PERFORM

       *> Nested loop control
       PERFORM VARYING OUTER-IDX FROM 1 BY 1 UNTIL OUTER-IDX > 10
           PERFORM VARYING INNER-IDX FROM 1 BY 1 UNTIL INNER-IDX > 10
               IF MATRIX(OUTER-IDX INNER-IDX) = 0 THEN
                   EXIT PERFORM INNER          @r{Exit inner loop only}
               END-IF
           END-PERFORM
       END-PERFORM
@end example

@node Dialogue
@section Calling a dialogue script (Forth scripts)

COBOL does not directly call Forth dialogue scripts. Instead, it uses
its own procedures. However, through the semantic-sugar
layer, COBOL commands can be mapped to calls of the shared Fountain/Forth
runtime library:

@example
       *> Original COBOL code
       IF (X > 100) AND (X < 200) THEN
           CALL "SHOW-MESSAGE" USING MSG-WELCOME
       END-IF

       *> Desugared via semantic-sugar layer
       IF ((X > 100) AND (X < 200)) THEN
           (:call-acc :target "ShowWelcomeMessage" :using nil)
       END-IF
@end example

@node Tiles
@section Manipulating tiles

Tile manipulation is performed through direct video memory access
or through library routines for tiled backgrounds:

@example
       *> Direct memory access (mode-dependent)
       MOVE &HB800 TO VIDEO-SEGMENT
       MOVE (OFFSET * 2) TO VIDEO-OFFSET
       MOVE TILE-NUM TO VIDEO-MEMORY(VIDEO-OFFSET)
       MOVE ATTRIBUTE TO VIDEO-MEMORY(VIDEO-OFFSET + 1)

       *> Library routine approach
       CALL "LOAD-TILESET" USING TILESET-ID
       CALL "PLACE-TILE" USING LAYER X Y TILE-INDEX
       CALL "SCROLL-BACKGROUND" USING DX DY
@end example

@node Decals
@section Manipulating decals

Decals (sprites or movable objects) are manipulated using:

@example
       *> Hardware sprite approach (if available)
       CALL "INIT-SPRITES"
       CALL "SET-SPRITE-POSITION" USING SPRITE-ID X Y
       CALL "SET-SPRITE-IMAGE" USING SPRITE-ID IMAGE-ID
       CALL "SHOW-SPRITE" USING SPRITE-ID -1   @r{-1 = visible}

       *> Software sprite approach
       01  SPRITE-TABLE.
           05  SPRITE-ENTRY OCCURS 10 TIMES.
               10  X-COORD PIC S9(3).
               10  Y-COORD PIC S9(3).
               10  FRAME   PIC 9.

       MOVE 100 TO X-COORD OF SPRITE-ENTRY(I)
       MOVE 50  TO Y-COORD OF SPRITE-ENTRY(I)
       COMPUTE FRAME OF SPRITE-ENTRY(I) = 
           (FRAME OF SPRITE-ENTRY(I) + 1) REM 4
@end example

@node Objects
@section Manipulating objects

Object manipulation depends on the implementation approach:

@example
       *> Using user-defined types (OBJECT COBOL)
       01  GAME-OBJECT.
           05  X                 PIC S9(3).
           05  Y                 PIC S9(3).
           05  WIDTH             PIC 9(2).
           05  HEIGHT            PIC 9(2).
           05  ACTIVE            PIC 9   VALUE 1.

       *> Initialize object
       MOVE 100 TO X OF GAME-OBJECT(I)
       MOVE 100 TO Y OF GAME-OBJECT(I)
       MOVE 16 TO WIDTH OF GAME-OBJECT(I)
       MOVE 16 TO HEIGHT OF GAME-OBJECT(I)
       MOVE 1   TO ACTIVE OF GAME-OBJECT(I)

       *> Update object position
       COMPUTE X OF GAME-OBJECT(I) = X OF GAME-OBJECT(I) + DX
       COMPUTE Y OF GAME-OBJECT(I) = Y OF GAME-OBJECT(I) + DY

       *> Check active status
       IF ACTIVE OF GAME-OBJECT(I) NOT = 0 THEN
           CALL "DRAW-OBJECT" USING GAME-OBJECT(I)
       END-IF
@end example

@node Unsupported
@section Unsupported language features

The following COBOL features are not supported in this EIGHTBOL release:

@table @asis
@item File I/O operations (OPEN, CLOSE, READ, WRITE, REWRITE, START)
@item Screen Section and ACCEPT/DISPLAY for formatted screens
@item Sorting and merging (SORT, MERGE)
@item Report Writer (RD, REPORT, INITIATE, GENERATE, TERMINATE)
@item Inter-program communication (COMMUNICATION, MESSAGE)
@item Object-oriented features beyond basic classes and instances
@item Pointer data types and procedure pointers
@item Floating-point arithmetic (unless emulated via library)
@item Intrinsic functions beyond basic math and string operations
@item Debugging and declaratives (USE FOR DEBUGGING, DECLARATIVES)
@end table

